# Prestige Skincare at Sephora

## Project Overview

This project explores what differentiates prestige skincare products on Sephora using Python, statistical analysis, and data visualization.

Rather than assuming higher prices indicate better products, the analysis investigates whether pricing, product characteristics, ingredients, categories, or brands explain customer satisfaction and consumer attention.

## Business Questions

1. Does paying more lead to higher customer satisfaction?
2. Do consumers pay more attention to expensive products?
3. Do objective product characteristics explain consumer perception?
4. Does brand matter more than product characteristics?

## Dataset

- ~1,500 prestige skincare products from Sephora
- Variables include:
  - Price
  - Rating
  - Sephora Loves
  - Brand
  - Product category
  - Ingredient list

## Methods

- Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)
- Pearson correlation
- Linear regression
- Mann–Whitney U tests
- Benjamini–Hochberg multiple testing correction
- Brand and category fixed-effects regression

## Key Findings

- Product price has virtually no relationship with customer ratings.
- Higher-priced products receive only slightly more consumer attention.
- Product categories explain relatively little variation in consumer outcomes.
- Ingredient families are much more strongly associated with pricing than with customer ratings or attention.
- Brand explains substantially more variation in pricing, ratings, and consumer attention than product category.

## Tools

- Python
- pandas
- NumPy
- matplotlib
- scipy
- statsmodels
- Google Colab

